ncing off the outer surface. The bright interior light and the dim reflection are two different things using the same pane of glass. A screen works exactly the same way: - **Transmitted light** = the image (RGB sub-pixels shining through the glass). - **Reflected light** = the distracting glare (room light bouncing off the outer glass surface). So your original intuition about reflection in the physical world still stands for objects. The screen just cheats by placing the *light source right behind the glass*, so the light you perceive as "yellow" is fresh, direct, additive light—not sunlight that bounced off a painted wall. The only "reflection" is an unwanted extra, not the mechanism for creating color.
